            "width": 87.14596949891067,
            "height": 8.82225100810356,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>Texte d'information (partie 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nWalter Brattain was born in China in 1902, where his parents worked as educators. His family later moved to the United States, where he received his schooling. Brattain showed early interest in mathematics and natural sciences. He studied physics at the University of Oregon and completed his master's degree at the University of Minnesota. His enthusiasm and talent for scientific research became evident quickly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Career Beginnings</strong><br>\nAfter completing his degree, Brattain started working as a physicist at Bell Laboratories, a leading research center where many significant inventions were made. Here, he focused on the study of solid-state physics, laying the groundwork for his later work that led to the invention of the transistor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration with John Bardeen and William Shockley</strong><br>\nAt Bell Labs, Brattain collaborated with other prominent scientists, including John Bardeen and William Shockley. The three formed a team that tackled challenges in solid-state physics and electronics. Their collaboration was crucial for the development of new technologies.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
