          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Discovery of the Transistor Effect</strong><br>\nThe discovery of the transistor was the result of intense research and experiments by Brattain and his team in the late 1940s. They found that certain semiconductor materials could be used to amplify electrical signals. This discovery was revolutionary and had the potential to reshape the electronics industry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Significance of the Transistor</strong><br>\nThe transistor was a pioneering invention, as it was smaller, more reliable, and more energy-efficient than the vacuum tubes used until then. Transistors quickly found widespread applications in radios, computers, and many other electronic devices. Their invention is considered one of the most important contributions of the 20th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nFor their contribution to the invention of the transistor, Brattain, Bardeen, and Shockley received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956. This recognition highlighted the significance of their work and its impact on modern electronics and science.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
